sd2snes_gbc -- Game Boy Color core
==================================

Licence
-------
GPL-2.0-only, like the rest of the sd2snes FPGA sources.


Origin
------
This core is derived from the Super Game Boy core of the sd2snes project
(GPLv2), which implements the SGB2 cartridge: the SM83 CPU, PPU, APU, timers,
serial port and the cartridge mappers.  Those files are carried over here
essentially unchanged.

What is new in this core is the SNES-facing half.  The SGB's ICD2 chip
(sgb_icd2.v), which packed the Game Boy's 2-bit pixel output into scanline
buffers for the SNES boot ROM to fetch, is replaced by:

  gbc_clk.v     Game Boy clock-enable generation
  gbc_bridge.v  the SNES bridge: read-only views over the Game Boy's own state
                in cartridge banks $E0-$E3, and byte-wide commands in $EF00xx

The picture is re-rendered on the SNES side from those views instead of being
composed in the FPGA.  The protocol between the two halves is specified in
GBC-CORE-CONTRACT.md.

  c6_fb.v       (wire $03) the optional framebuffer mode: the PPU's own CGB
                pixel, quantised to SNES direct colour, kept in block RAM and
                read by the SNES as 8bpp tiles in bank $E4 -- for the scenes
                whose per-line palette traffic the view/log path cannot carry.

  c6_sram.v     (Mk.II, wire $04) stores the same quantised 160x144 pixels in
                external SRAM at $70000-$78fff. A 2 KiB planar row cache
                serves native or 5:8 stretched SNES DMA reads. The player
                writes row 0..17 to $EF0008, waits for status +$92 to equal
                that row, then uses the existing $E4 view and ROW_DONE.
                Status +$93 bit 0 reports a sticky producer FIFO overflow;
                overflow invalidates the cache rather than exposing torn data.

The GBC configuration contains no SA-1 instance. It also no longer contains
MSU-1 hardware: the data/register interface, PCM buffer/interpolator, mixer
and MCU controls have been removed. dac.v retains the GB APU's original CIC
filter, volume boost, saturation and I2S serializer on both boards.

Mk.II status
------------
This is an experimental port, excluded from normal Mk.II packages and MCU
routing until native implementation and console validation are complete.
The original SGB core remains the normal Mk.II Game Boy core. An explicit
MCU build with MK2_GBC_EXPERIMENTAL=1 enables GBC routing and uses a separate
obj-mk2-gbc directory, so it cannot reuse production objects. The GBC route
never selects the SGB/MSU variant. The Mk.II GBC port keeps both VRAM banks,
the colour pipeline, HDMA and double speed. Small
memories use LUT RAM; the optional MCU state-debug window is omitted there.
Wire $04 also omits status counters unused by the SNES player: +$0D, +$59,
+$5E, and the +$30..+$3F debug mirror read zero, except +$3C/+$3D retain
the bus-starvation counter for board validation. The functional SEQ,
LOG_OVF, row-freeze, dirty, palette and cache-ready/overflow fields remain.
COLW_N saturates at 256 on Mk.II: the player tests only >=96 and >=256,
so additional count precision cannot change its rendering-mode policy.
The player image must remain below SRAM $70000, reserved for video storage.

Build and verify on GitHub Actions (Build firmware, FPGA mode):
  fpga_cores = mk2:gbc,mk3:gbc
  ignore_timing = false
The explicit mk2:gbc selector runs the experimental ISE build without adding
it to production inventories. The same run executes tests/test_gbc_mk2.py,
assembles both player variants, and runs the existing SNES-player tests.
It uploads native resource/timing reports and the two player images.
Wire $04 uses gbc_snes_mk2.bin; wire $03 keeps gbc_snes.bin. A mismatched pair
fails the existing version check. No additional image quantisation is used.
The optional gbc_mcu_check input builds a real Mk.II bootstrap, then compiles
and links production and experimental MCU firmware in separate object trees.
These checks do not qualify a GBC bitstream or replace console validation.
The optional gbc_yosys_check input runs an independent Spartan-3 synthesis
mapping diagnostic. Combine it with gbc_rtl_only=all to avoid a native build.
Its resource estimate includes ten RAMB16s inside the unchanged CoreGen
wrappers. It does not enable production routing or publish a bitstream.


Block RAM rules
---------------
The EP4CE15 has 56 M9K and this core uses 53 of them.  Any new memory follows
the two rules the framebuffer fit established (c6_fb.v header):

  * a RAM deeper than 512 words gets maximum_depth = 512 on its altsyncram,
    or the fitter builds it from 1K x 8 blocks and wastes a third of them;
  * a small array read by index (a few dozen entries) gets ramstyle = "logic",
    or each one is placed in a block of its own.


